  "transcript": "# Multilateralism\n\n## Who should set AI rules?\n> Relying only on advanced powers to set AI norms risks leaving many countries behind and creating instability.\n\nChair: Please introduce yourself and tell us whether it's sensible for technologically advanced powers to set global AI rules for everyone.\nDr Rachel Adams: I'm Dr Rachel Adams, Executive Director at Cambridge's Leverhulme Centre and founder of the Global Center on AI Governance, which supports Global South governments.\nDr Rachel Adams: Having only a few advanced countries set norms is risky. Those rules may not suit other countries and can block research and cooperation—GDPR is an example.\nDr Rachel Adams: We're in a multipolar world. If only some nations shape AI law, other regions will be left behind and tensions about control and values will grow.\n\n\n## What's happening in the Global South?\n> Many developing countries are publishing AI frameworks, but most are non‑binding and lack enforcement or resources.\n\nBaroness Fraser of Craigmaddie: Do governments in the Global South feel left out, and what do they care about most?\nDr Rachel Adams: They do feel excluded from global rule‑making. Our Global Index on Responsible AI shows recent growth: over half of new frameworks come from the Global South.\nDr Rachel Adams: But 78% of those frameworks are non‑binding—they show intent but usually lack enforcement, oversight bodies or budgets to implement protections.\nDr Rachel Adams: Implementation also lags: roughly 45% of Global South frameworks are being implemented versus about 65% in the global North, though many are new.\nDr Rachel Adams: The risks countries prioritise differ. Rich countries worry about frontier and existential risks; many Global South concerns are immediate: labour, weak institutions, and data sovereignty.\nDr Rachel Adams: Examples: underpaid data‑labelling work with no labour protections, overwhelmed human rights bodies handling complaints about new data centres, and unequal environmental impacts.\nDr Rachel Adams: Public understanding is low. In South Africa two‑thirds of people we surveyed didn't know what AI was; those who did relied on platforms for information, which affects democratic accountability.\n\n\n## Who is helping and where are the gaps?\n> There are multiple international initiatives, but coordination, regional tailoring and follow‑through are weak.\n\nBaroness Blackstone: Some say middle‑income countries are a US‑China battleground. Is that how they see it?\nDr Rachel Adams: That's a Western framing. African governments don't mainly see themselves as pawns in a binary US‑China contest. They want practical partnerships and agency.\nDr Rachel Adams: US influence now often flows through big tech, while China offers accessible open models and training. Both have advantages and limits, depending on local needs.\nDr Rachel Adams: On multilateral mechanisms: UNESCO's ethical framework and readiness tool were useful but relied on global indicators that don't always include many countries.\nDr Rachel Adams: The UN Global Dialogue is inclusive, but with thousands of short statements it's hard to reach concrete commitments or follow up on promises.\nDr Rachel Adams: China's WAICO launched in 2026 emphasised development and training and has some African signatories; it's early to judge its impact.\nDr Rachel Adams: The G20 is valuable—South Africa used it well—but global processes need links to regional bodies like the African Union and to national regulators so commitments turn into action.\n\n\n## Diffusion of AI and capacity building\n> Technology adoption is uneven and many development efforts succeed only when they support local priorities and institutions.\n\nLord Darroch of Kew: Microsoft found 25% AI use in workplaces in the global North versus 14% in the Global South—how effective have international initiatives been at spreading capability?\nDr Rachel Adams: Such stats are useful but often model formal economies; they miss informal work common in many countries. Diffusion doesn't automatically mean fair benefits.\nDr Rachel Adams: The AI for Development initiative (originating with Canada and Sweden, with FCDO support) has helped universities and projects across Africa, producing useful local innovations.\nDr Rachel Adams: Successful programmes focus on local priorities, strengthen democratic institutions, and build university capacity—these are long‑term, not quick‑win, investments.\nDr Rachel Adams: Many funders and tech firms favour fast scaling. But without institution building and citizen engagement, gains aren't sustained or equitable.\n\n\n## What can the UK realistically do?\n> The UK should use diplomatic tools—training, scholarships, and G20 leadership—rather than only money or infrastructure, to support Global South agency.\n\nBaroness Crawley: How can the UN processes be shaped so the Global South really buys in? And about WAICO: are its African signatories long‑standing Chinese partners?\nDr Rachel Adams: For the UN, make it practical: link global talks to regional bodies, national regulators and funding so commitments become concrete and trackable.\nDr Rachel Adams: I can't name the ten WAICO signatories offhand. It's important to study their ties to China, but irrespective of origins, these initiatives matter because they offer political voice and training.\nLord Bruce of Bennachie: With the UK's aid cuts and fewer diplomats, is the UK credible in offering meaningful engagement?\nDr Rachel Adams: Yes—credibility can come from non‑expensive diplomatic tools. The UK should fund AI diplomacy training, use Chevening scholarships, and support university partnerships.\nDr Rachel Adams: Strengthen institutions in partner countries: competition authorities, data protection agencies, human rights bodies and gender commissions—help them participate fully in global talks.\nDr Rachel Adams: The UK has world‑class universities and global diplomatic reach; using those for training diplomats and regulators is high value and relatively low cost.\n\n\n## Top practical recommendations\n> Make global processes result in tracked, funded action; train diplomats and regulators; use the G20 and UK universities to build Global South agency.\n\nChair: What's your top recommendation to the British Government?\nDr Rachel Adams: Use the G20 to revive commitments from South Africa's presidency, especially on labour, unequal access to compute and tech's social harms—bring those into concrete follow‑up actions.\nDr Rachel Adams: Second, the UK should anchor an AI diplomacy programme—train diplomats and regulators from the Global South so they can engage and negotiate effectively.\nDr Rachel Adams: Finally, track commitments across forums. When companies and states pledge training, money or capacity building, there must be a way to see whether resources and training really flow.\nChair: Thank you, Dr Adams. We appreciate your time and these concrete suggestions.\n",
